<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#sugar-plantation">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sugar plantation</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as sugar-plantation, sugar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A sugar plantation is a large agricultural estate growing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a> for sugar production, typically using intensive labour. From the fifteenth century such plantations, worked by enslaved Africans, spread from Atlantic islands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. They were central to the Portuguese, French and Brazilian empires, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> later developed sugar production in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>.</p></div>
